{% if not enabled %}
    <div class="streaming-video-env">
        <span class="status grey"></span>
        <div>
            <strong>Switched off.</strong>
            Set <code>{{ envVar }}=true</code> on the app and redeploy: on the craft-php image the same variable installs FFmpeg in the worker. Until then nothing is encoded and <code>hlsPlaylistUrl</code> is always <code>null</code>, so the frontend plays the original files.
        </div>
    </div>
{% else %}
    <div class="streaming-video-env">
        <span class="status green"></span>
        <div>
            <strong>On</strong> (<code>{{ envVar }}=true</code>). New and replaced videos are encoded in the queue. FFmpeg runs in the queue worker: if it's missing there, videos fail with "FFmpeg/FFprobe not found".
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="streaming-video-counts">
        {% for status in statuses %}
            <div class="streaming-video-count">
                <strong>{{ statusCounts[status.value] ?? 0 }}</strong>
                <span class="status {{ status.color() }}"></span> {{ status.label() }}
            </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>

    <form method="post" accept-charset="UTF-8">
        {{ csrfInput() }}
        {{ actionInput('toolkit/streaming-video/queue') }}
        {{ redirectInput(redirectPath) }}

        <div class="field">
            <button type="submit" class="btn submit">Queue videos</button>
            <p class="light">Queues only the videos switched on that need it: never encoded, replaced, failed, or encoded with other settings. Each video is encoded once per quality, so many or long videos keep the worker busy for a long time and use a lot of disk.</p>
        </div>
    </form>

    <hr>
    <h3>Streaming field</h3>
    <p class="light">Volumes with the Streaming field can stream their videos: it shows on video files only, with Convert to HLS off until an editor switches it on. <code>media</code> gets it on its own while streaming is on.</p>
    <table class="data fullwidth">
        <tbody>
            {% for row in volumeStatuses %}
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>{{ row.volume.name }}</strong> <span class="light"><code>{{ row.volume.handle }}</code></span></td>
                    <td class="thin nowrap">
                        {% if row.hasField %}
                            <span class="status green"></span> In the layout
                        {% elseif allowAdminChanges %}
                            <form method="post" accept-charset="UTF-8">
                                {{ csrfInput() }}
                                {{ actionInput('toolkit/streaming-video/add-field') }}
                                {{ redirectInput(redirectPath) }}
                                {{ hiddenInput('volumeId', row.volume.id) }}
                                <button type="submit" class="btn small">Add field</button>
                            </form>
                        {% else %}
                            <span class="status grey"></span> Not in the layout: add it in development and deploy
                        {% endif %}
                    </td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    {% if failures is not empty %}
        <hr>
        <h3>Failed videos</h3>
        <p class="light">Fix the cause, then queue the videos again. Files that aren't MP4/MOV, MKV/WebM or MPEG-TS are refused on purpose: convert them to MP4.</p>
        <table class="data fullwidth">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Video</th>
                    <th>Error</th>
                    <th>When</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                {% for failure in failures %}
                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            {% if failure.asset %}
                                <a href="{{ failure.asset.cpEditUrl }}">{{ failure.asset.title }}</a>
                            {% else %}
                                #{{ failure.assetId }}
                            {% endif %}
                        </td>
                        <td><div class="streaming-video-error">{{ failure.error ?? '—' }}</div></td>
                        <td class="nowrap">{{ failure.dateUpdated|datetime('short') }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% endfor %}
            </tbody>
        </table>
    {% endif %}
{% endif %}

<hr>

<h3>In the frontend</h3>
<p class="light">Query <code>hlsPlaylistUrl</code> and <code>videoPoster</code> next to <code>url</code>: <code>&lt;CraftMedia&gt;</code> from <code>@studio-fes/layer-craft</code> plays HLS when it's there and the original file otherwise. The Help tab has the fields to create, the fragment and the options.</p>

<h3>Checklist</h3>
<ul class="bullets">
    <li>The video volume's layout has a Streaming Video field, and the videos to stream have it switched on.</li>
    <li>The video volume's Base URL is absolute (e.g. <code>$ASSETS_BASE_URL</code>), or the playlist URL points to the frontend's domain.</li>
    <li>The <code>.m3u8</code> and <code>.ts</code> files are served with CORS headers. The craft-php image already does it; on a bucket or CDN, allow <code>GET</code> from the frontend.</li>
    <li>The frontend's GraphQL schema can query assets in the video volume.</li>
    <li>The PHP upload limit is the maximum video size (300M on craft-php: raise <code>PHP_POST_MAX_SIZE</code> and <code>PHP_UPLOAD_MAX_FILE_SIZE</code>).</li>
    <li>On craft-php, <code>web</code> and <code>worker</code> mount the same uploads volume: the worker writes the files, nginx serves them.</li>
</ul>
